UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 1: Combining skills in a mini game', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA mini game ends with arguments about who scored. Add one rule or role that makes scoring fair.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan and play a mini game that combines at least three skills from this unit. Write the goal, rules, boundary, scoring and safety rules, then play two rounds. Record one change you made after round one and whether it helped.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan and play a mini game that combines at least three skills from this unit. Write the goal, rules, boundary, scoring and safety rules, then play two rounds. Record one change you made after round one and whether it hel Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner who catches it and places it in a hoop. Rules: walk when carrying the ball; wait your turn; 1 point per delivery. After round 1 they widen the hoop to make scoring more even.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Correction: Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game.","Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and demonstrate\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the reasoning\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Leave someone out because the game felt easier that way."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Leave someone out because the game felt easier that way.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new situation\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair.","Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"A mini game ends with arguments about who scored. Add one rule or role that make\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\". Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair.","Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people. Useful feedback uses the original cues, rules and reasons.","options":["The new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a sh\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named cues, rules or choices.","question":"Describe or practise a fresh situation that uses the central skill. Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Example mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner who catches it and places it in a hoop. Rules: walk when carrying the ball; wait your turn; 1 point per delivery. After round 1 they widen the hoop to make scoring more even.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the choice or strategy, not just make the answer longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus a corrected choice and a reason linked to safety, health or respect.","question":"Name one likely misconception or unsafe choice for this topic, correct it and say why the correction is safer, healthier or more respectful.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Correction: Add a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w10-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a mini game.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use a mini game with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify combining skills and explain why it matters.","I can practise a boundary independently on a new prompt.","I can notice changing a rule needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner who catches it and places it in a hoop. Rules: walk when carrying the ball; wait your turn; 1 point per delivery. After round 1 they widen the hoop to make scoring more even.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA mini game ends with arguments about who scored. Add one rule or role that makes scoring fair.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair.","explain":"A mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A mini game with three skills.","caption":"Skills plus rules.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Skills</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">run, bounce, pass, catch</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rules</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">walk with ball, wait</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, p Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, p Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner who catches it and places it in a hoop. Rules: walk when carrying the ball; wait your turn; 1 point per delivery. After round 1 they widen the hoop to make scoring more even. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Changing a rule after play.","caption":"Improve one rule.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Round 1</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">arguments about score</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Round 2</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">scorekeeper counts aloud</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Here is a more helpful example: Example mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner who catches it and places it in a hoop. Rules: walk when carrying the ball; wait your turn; 1 point per delivery. After round 1 they widen the hoop to make scoring more even.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAdd a scorekeeper who counts out loud, or agree that a goal counts only when the ball is in the hoop. Clear scoring rules prevent arguments and keep the game fair.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a mini game is only about winning. A good game is safe, fair and fun for all players, and winning is just one part. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA mini game combines skills such as running, throwing, catching, bouncing and kicking, and adds rules, space and a purpose. Good mini games have a clear goal, simple rules, a safe boundary, fair teams and a way for everyone to take part. After playing, players review what worked and change a rule to improve the game. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 10, Lesson 1: Combining skills in a mini game","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Fundamental movement skills","week":10,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample mini game ''Collect and deliver'': in a marked 10 metre square, players run to get a soft ball, bounce it twice, pass to a partner who catches it and places it in a hoop. Rules: walk when carrying the ball; wait your turn; 1 point per delivery. After round 1 they widen the hoop to make scoring more even.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A mini game with three skills.","caption":"Skills plus rules.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Skills</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">run, bounce, pass, catch</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rules</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">walk with ball, wait</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Changing a rule after play.","caption":"Improve one rule.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><tex